Relative indexes
description: the dot in the brackets - the host frame, aligned by depth
- >
  A dot in the brackets makes the index **relative to the pointer's own position**: `[.]` is "my
  position at this depth", `[.-1]` one before me, `[.+2]` two after. The `[.±k]` bracket survived
  the YAML-keys round unchanged - it is a non-literal position operator, not an index literal (an
  absolute position is a bare-integer segment), and it is the **one surviving bracket operator**:
  the former `[]` append and `[?]` wildcard brackets were removed - both are the `-` segment now.
  A bare `-1` segment is deliberately not taken as a key, kept free for a possible future
  from-the-end index. Formally: `relindex = "[" "." [("+"/"-") 1*DIGIT] "]"`, attached to the
  segment before it.
- >
  Resolution is the **frame rule**: the frame is the host entry's own path - the entry holding the
  pointer. After the base and any `..` ascents, each step consumes one depth, and a `[.±k]` at depth d
  selects the host's position at that depth, offset by k. Keyed entries hold positions too, so the
  frame always exists.
- !!yo
  - *[.-1]        # the cell to my LEFT - a colspan
  - *..[.-1][.]   # the cell ABOVE: up to the table, previous
                  #   row, my column - a rowspan
- >
  The motivating idiom is the table - a merged cell names its neighbor. A relative index yields at
  most one successor, so it is a **link**, authorable after `*`; out of range is the ordinary
  dangling diagnostic. In bookmarks it is **rejected** - a position claim is outlawed, and a
  relative claim is still a claim - and in queries too: a query has no host frame.
